I'm joined today by Luke Vargas he is  the United Nations bureau chief for talk  media news he's joined us from all over  the world and today he is joining us  from Mexico City where he's been  spending a week learning about trade  immigration relations with the United  States let's start with the feeling  there Luke in terms of what Donald Trump  has been saying possible tariffs a wall  that he says at some point somehow  Mexico will be paying for what's the  general feeling there about the Trump  rhetoric here's a statistic that has  blew my mind and will probably blow  yours as well right before the election  public opinion firms here in Mexico  pulled the country and found that around  11 percent of Mexicans felt that the  us-mexico relationship was poor or very  poor that number is now close to 50% so  you've seen a tremendous change in  public opinion in this country moved  shifting negative about the United  States and that 11 percent is worth just  emphasizing it's near historic lows I  mean there is not a better time for  relations between these two countries  and that has changed dramatically it's  so bad now that I spoke to the CEO of a  leading market research firm here the  other day who said there is this  long-standing phenomenon in Mexico  called Malin cheese MOU it goes back a  long time and it's sort of it's it  explains sort of a preference on the  part of Mexicans for foreign products  and US brands have always been very  popular here and this man said the shift  is now very happening very rapidly and  whether consciously formally or  subconsciously informally Mexicans are  now turning away from American brands  and that's very important because you  have companies like Walmart making  billions of dollars a month here 711s on  every corner Home Depot's Ashleigh  furnitures you name it  Mexico for many American companies is  the second biggest overseas market for  them and for American public  you know Mexican public opinions to  change about the government but also for  American corporations is going to  undermine this America first narrative  that Donald Trump has been talking about  so from the newspapers to public opinion  there has been a big shift here people  just are  not having it at all with the rhetoric  of Donald Trump and what about in terms  of relations between the governments we  found that much of what Donald Trump  said he would be doing with relation to  the the Mexican government during the  campaign has not actually come to  fruition we've seen a meeting scheduled  and then cancelled between Donald Trump  and the Mexican president how are things  behind the scenes diplomatically if it's  possible to even know that I think there  was a hope and this is a hope shared on  the part of many people in the US and  around the world that Donald Trump might  be saying one thing but the people who  are actually implementing his decisions  would be on a very different page the  concern here in Mexico is really that  the bottom is going to fall out of this  relationship and in many ways it Mexican  government officials and legislators are  being pushed to do things which they  believe are going to harm Mexico when  you have 50% of the countries saying  they think relations are bad with the  United States and probably even higher  numbers saying they don't like the  government of Donald Trump then those  legislators are forced to introduce  bills interrupting the relations between  these two countries in a manner of ways  so that is one thing that is driving  these two countries apart you've seen  the threat of suspending law enforcement  cooperation between Mexico and the  United States another anecdote that I  think is very important to keep in mind  here is that in 2014 President Obama  asked Enrique Pena Nieto the Mexican  president to crack down on Mexico's  southern border essentially if Mexico  could keep Central American migrants and  asylum seekers out of Mexico in the  first place they wouldn't then be  traveling on to the United States and  Pena Nieto complied deportations I  believe were up to about a quarter  million last year alone and he risked  relations Pena Nieto with his neighbors  in order to do this thinking that the  administration in Washington would pay  him back for that and I'm sure Hillary  Clinton super president would have done  so there is now such a feeling of being  spurned that not only is this  administration not thanking Mexico for  probably keeping the number of people  passing into the United States lower  than it otherwise would have been  but Mexico is now singularly being  blamed for this very crisis so I think  relations are really souring on all  manner of fronts and it's so bad now I  spoke  to some financial advisors here who say  the u.s. is turning into a Banana  Republic  they're telling their Mexican  billionaire investors not even to bother  with the United States for the next four  years we've heard of CEOs having their  social media accounts looked into at  airports combing through emails  we even had a NASA scientist who had his  phone taken at an airport  obviously not Mexican in that case but  you know this is this is so bad now that  the the warning coming from those in  power here in Mexico - you know wealthy  folks - other members of the governments  to CEOs is don't bother with the United  States Mexico perhaps is the country  that will pick up the mantle of freedom  of expression freedom of trade but for  now we just do not trust the United  States at its core which is a very big  shift for such an important alliance  yeah I mean along those lines you have  some trumpets in the US who see the  so-called crackdown on Mexico as  something that will just be good for the  United States bad for Mexico period and  this sort of very black and white  clearly delineated a set of consequences  but it actually could be bad for both  Mexico and the US for the relationship  to go south couldn't it yes from Mexico  the financial toll is could be  devastating I think it's 73 percent of  exports from this country are bound for  the United States so any disruption in  that however minimal and looks like it  probably won't be minimal is going to  have a big impact then you start  thinking about people coming home if  there are large waves of deportees  arriving here back in Mexico that is  going to destabilize an economy that has  been on a very good trajectory in recent  years and that causes a problem which is  felt on both sides of the border which  is that you know crime has been on the  decrease here you have seen you know  good attempts in some states bad  attempts and others but generally a  correlation between low unemployment in  Mexico and a reduction in crime and if  you start to see hundreds of thousands  of people returning to a job market that  really doesn't have a lot of need for  more workers the number of opportunities  decreases and people may find themselves  looking for other types of informal work  or even you know becoming curious and  criminal and  surprises and that is something very  destabilizing for the United States  first what the US has to lose it's not  only that law enforcement cooperation in  barrel mine you know Mexican visa and  immigration policy as well as refugee  policy has often tracked that at the  United States for a reason so that the  United States could never say oh you  know terrorists could just be wandering  over the border because Mexico doesn't  let people in from many countries around  the world that the u.s. also is worried  about but of course now we've seen  Donald Trump saying terrorists could  just be wandering over the border so  Mexico is saying what is the point of  this cooperation and there are other and  just as one more quick anecdote about  how this could hurt the United States  look at an industry like corn I think  this has been talked about quite a lot  but Donald Trump always focuses on the  Rust Belt states bringing back  manufacturing bringing back 19th century  energy jobs in Appalachia and here we  have the prospect that the Mexican  legislature could pass a bill banning  imports of corn from the United States  well that this you know Mexico is the  largest export market for American corn  27% of all exports if that goes away you  have a new geographic region a new  economic portion of the United States  economy that could be adversely affected  and give Trump just another economic  challenge to have to confront so the  ramifications here just ripple out in so  many different ways look in the limited  time we have left nafta okay we've heard  from Trump a whole you know there's been  a whole spectrum of claims about what  he's going to do with regard to NAFTA  and Mexico  what what's really on the table right  now in terms of the NAFTA talks  well Mexico says they're open to  renegotiation I think they just want to  be seen as being a good faith negotiator  that they're never going to just oppose  something you know full-stop what's on  the table they don't they're not  interested in tariffs that's completely  off the table one area where I think  there is some flexibility is labor  standards wages these are things they  were going to sign up for anyways with  the trans-pacific partnership of which  they were a member during the  negotiation so they're probably willing  to take that language in maybe re  incorporate it into it you know another  NAFTA reboot also there's something  called rules of origin sixty two point  five percent of a product if it's made  within the NAFTA zone the product will  then be exempt from tariffs there is a  push in Washington to raise that perhaps  to 80 percent perhaps more Mexico would  like this in some ways because it could  mean that supply chains that perhaps  aren't a part of Mexico or North America  could come into this country but they're  nervous that the u.s. might push it that  they might say maybe 90% and in that  case corporations that rely on Mexico is  a key part of their supply chain might  not you just say attack with it let's  just do all our operations in Bangladesh  and maybe finish the product in the  United States and bypass Mexico so  they're willing on one hand but also  very nervous because this is a Pandora's  box you open up and after the process  drags on everybody wants input in this  agreement could splinter off into so  many different directions deportations  Trump I don't know that Donald Trump is  actually going to increase the  importation is relative to the Obama  administration in fact the plan that  represented at one point would would  extrapolate it out have a few a smaller  number of deportations happen under  Trump than what we saw under President  Obama but hypothetically if Trump were  to drastically increase deportations  from the Obama era what would that do in  Mexico  well and I think we are seeing with the  latest DHS instructions to the agency  that they are going to view all 11  million undocumented immigrants as a  priority now so those promises from  Trump may be off the table at this point  that we do need to wait and see there  are some sectors in this economy and  this is just a small caveat that  actually say they'd like some more  deport disease to come back people with  good bilingual skills people with other  various skills to go into the call  center industry to go into tourism to be  on cruise ships there's a desire for  those Mexicans who are in the u.s. to  come back home but that employment base  is going to be tapped out relatively  quickly there was an editorial here just  the other day that said if 10% of the  six million undocumented Mexicans came  back to Mexico it would inundate  government services and civil social  services we are perhaps looking at a  number much greater than that and this  government simply doesn't have a plan  pain a Nieto came in and was a president  seen by many as a puppet who would have  done well harmonizing relations with  Hillary Clinton you know keep everything  nice and easy going down the course on  the status quo he's not a president in  many people's eyes who is going to be  able to forcefully confront Donald Trump  someone who's going to be able to  radically reengineer the Mexican economy  to accept in all these people and  there's great concern that if all those  workers come back unemployment goes up  wages start going down and you have a  crisis in Mexico that may not be just an  immigration crisis but perhaps a refugee  crisis gone for bid on you know our  southern border which obviously the  consequences are very grave luke vargas  in Mexico City today United Nations  bureau chief for talk media news thanks  so much Luke thank you

